1.832.092-.647.35-1.088.636-1.338-2.22-.253-4.555-1.113-4.555-4.951 0-1.093.39-1.988 1.029-2.688-.103-.253-.446-1.272.098-2.65 0 0 .84-.27 2.75 1.026A9.564 9.564 0 0112 6.844c.85.004 1.705.115 2.504.337 1.909-1.296 2.747-1.027 2.747-1.027.546 1.379.202 2.398.1 2.651.64.7 1.028 1.595 1.028 2.688 0 3.848-2.339 4.695-4.566 4.943.359.309.678.92.678 1.855 0 1.338-.012 2.419-.012 2.747 0 .268.18.58.688.482A10.019 10.019 0 0022 12.017C22 6.484 17.522 2 12 2z\" clip-rule=\"evenodd\"></path></svg></a><a href=\"#\" class=\"text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-500 dark:hover:text-gray-200\"><span class=\"sr-only\">Dribbble</span><svg fill=\"currentColor\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" class=\"h-6 w-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path fill-rule=\"evenodd\" d=\"M12 2C6.48 2 2 6.48 2 12s4.48 10 10 10c5.51 0 10-4.48 10-10S17.51 2 12 2zm6.605 4.61a8.502 8.502 0 011.93 5.314c-.281-.054-3.101-.629-5.943-.271-.065-.141-.12-.293-.184-.445a25.416 25.416 0 00-.564-1.236c3.145-1.28 4.577-3.124 4.761-3.362zM12 3.475c2.17 0 4.154.813 5.662 2.148-.152.216-1.443 1.941-4.48 3.08-1.399-2.57-2.95-4.675-3.189-5A8.687 8.687 0 0112 3.475zm-3.633.803a53.896 53.896 0 013.167 4.935c-3.992 1.063-7.517 1.04-7.896 1.04a8.581 8.581 0 014.729-5.975zM3.453 12.01v-.26c.37.01 4.512.065 8.775-1.215.25.477.477.965.694 1.453-.109.033-.228.065-.336.098-4.404 1.42-6.747 5.303-6.942 5.629a8.522 8.522 0 01-2.19-5.705zM12 20.547a8.482 8.482 0 01-5.239-1.8c.152-.315 1.888-3.656 6.703-5.337.022-.01.033-.01.054-.022a35.318 35.318 0 011.823 6.475 8.4 8.4 0 01-3.341.684zm4.761-1.465c-.086-.52-.542-3.015-1.659-6.084 2.679-.423 5.022.271 5.314.369a8.468 8.468 0 01-3.655 5.715z\" clip-rule=\"evenodd\"></path></svg></a></div><p class=\"mt-8 text-center text-base text-gray-400 cursor-default\">© <!-- -->2026<!-- --> <span class=\"hover:text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r bg-gradient-to-r from-pink-500 via-red-500 to-yellow-500\">Next.js Blog Starter</span>, Inc. All rights reserved.</p></div></div></footer></div></div><script id=\"__NEXT_DATA__\" type=\"application/json\">{\"props\":{\"pageProps\":{\"post\":{\"title\":\"A blog starter you actually want to use\",\"date\":\"2021-08-24T05:35:07.322Z\",\"slug\":\"a-nextjs-blog-starter-you-actually-want-to-use\",\"author\":{\"name\":\"sandypockets\",\"picture\":\"/assets/blog/authors/sandypockets_avatar.jpg\"},\"content\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eThere are hundreds of Next.js blog starters out there. I've tinkered with a few dozen of them, and while there are many that are quite good, they just didn't seem to fit what I wanted to do with them. Nothing was ready out of the box. That's fine for some projects, but for others, I'm not looking to reinvent the wheel.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eI've always been drawn to the Next.js blog starter. The overall design is clean, and it supports markdown, though it doesn't come with much styling for it. But still lacked features, like Google Analytics, dark mode, or syntax highlighting, that I'd become accustomed to with my own bespoke templates. \\u003cem\\u003eThis\\u003c/em\\u003e starter, is the result of combining the features I've come to rely on with much of the OG Next blog starter's slick layout.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch2\\u003eFeatures ✨\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\n\\u003ch5\\u003eDark mode\\u003c/h5\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThe app defaults to the user's OS preferences. If the user hasn't selected an OS preference, the theme defaults to light mode. The user can switch between light and dark mode using the toggle in the nav. Dark/light theme preferences are stored on the client, in \\u003ccode\\u003elocalStorage.theme\\u003c/code\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAdding new dark classes is easy. Simply prefix the Tailwind CSS class name with \\u003ccode\\u003edark:\\u003c/code\\u003e and it will be applied during dark mode only.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch5\\u003eGoogle Analytics\\u003c/h5\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eUnderstanding traffic is a big part of blogging. Simply copy your Google ID, paste it into the \\u003ccode\\u003e.env.local\\u003c/code\\u003e file, and you're all set. The Google tag is set up to track a number of events, including form submissions.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch5\\u003eA working contact form\\u003c/h5\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThe contact form uses \\u003ca href=\\\"https://sendgrid.com/\\\"\\u003eSendGrid\\u003c/a\\u003e to send emails from your contact form on your behalf. Their free plan is suitable. Just add your SendGrid API key, and your \\\"to\\\" and \\\"from\\\" email addresses to the \\u003ccode\\u003e.env.local\\u003c/code\\u003e file, and you're all set. Submissions to the contact form will be emailed directly to your inbox. And, as mentioned above, all submissions on the contact form are tracked as Google Analytics events.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch5\\u003eMarkdown\\u003c/h5\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIt's a blog. We're here for the content. You shouldn't be wrangling HTML or CSS. You should be writing. Creating. That's it. And that's exactly what markdown lets you do. But, unlike many blog starters touting markdown, \\u003cem\\u003ethis\\u003c/em\\u003e starter actually comes with built-in styling. Take a look at the \\u003ca href=\\\"/posts/markdown-guide\\\"\\u003eMarkdown Guide\\u003c/a\\u003e for examples.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch5\\u003eSyntax highlighting\\u003c/h5\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIs your blog code heavy? Tutorials? All preformatted (\\u003ccode\\u003e\\u0026#x3C;pre\\u003e\\u003c/code\\u003e) code blocks are highlighted with Prism. The theme can be adjusted in the Meta component.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch5\\u003eTailwind CSS\\u003c/h5\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eTailwind keeps CSS manageable, in an understandable, and scalable way. If you're not already familiar with Tailwind, check it out \\u003ca href=\\\"https://tailwindcss.com\\\"\\u003ehere\\u003c/a\\u003e, and never look back.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch5\\u003eStorybook\\u003c/h5\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eStorybook helps you build more composable components, by crafting and testing them in isolation. Run \\u003ccode\\u003eyarn storybook\\u003c/code\\u003e from the root directory to start Storybook and view stories.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch5\\u003eStatically generated\\u003c/h5\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eNeed to show the same page to a bunch of users? Generate it at build. When your users ask for your content, deliver it lightning fast.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch5\\u003eImage optimization\\u003c/h5\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eDefer images you don't need, and prioritize the ones you do. All the while, making sure you're serving up the smallest size possible without sacrificing quality. For example, the hero image on the homepage receives priority (since it's above the fold), while the images below the fold, in the \\\"More stories\\\" section, will be deferred. The same is true for the hero image on each blog post's page.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ch5\\u003eReact\\u003c/h5\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eA fast, robust library. Popular enough that you'll always be able to find the tools and packages you need to build out custom features.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\",\"ogImage\":{\"url\":\"/assets/blog/a-nextjs-blog-starter-you-actually-want-to-use/tree.jpg\"},\"coverImage\":\"/assets/blog/a-nextjs-blog-starter-you-actually-want-to-use/tree-minimal.jpg\"}},\"__N_SSG\":true},\"page\":\"/posts/[slug]\",\"query\":{\"slug\":\"a-nextjs-blog-starter-you-actually-want-to-use\"},\"buildId\":\"EAmQPcL6IFOUF1wlMAJFx\",\"isFallback\":false,\"gsp\":true,\"scriptLoader\":[]}</script></body></html>","json":{"pageProps":{"post":{"title":"A blog starter you actually want to use","date":"2021-08-24T05:35:07.322Z","slug":"a-nextjs-blog-starter-you-actually-want-to-use","author":{"name":"sandypockets","picture":"/assets/blog/authors/sandypockets_avatar.jpg"},"content":"<p>There are hundreds of Next.js blog starters out there. I've tinkered with a few dozen of them, and while there are many that are quite good, they just didn't seem to fit what I wanted to do with them. Nothing was ready out of the box. That's fine for some projects, but for others, I'm not looking to reinvent the wheel.</p>\n<p>I've always been drawn to the Next.js blog starter. The overall design is clean, and it supports markdown, though it doesn't come with much styling for it. But still lacked features, like Google Analytics, dark mode, or syntax highlighting, that I'd become accustomed to with my own bespoke templates. <em>This</em> starter, is the result of combining the features I've come to rely on with much of the OG Next blog starter's slick layout.</p>\n<h2>Features ✨</h2>\n<h5>Dark mode</h5>\n<p>The app defaults to the user's OS preferences. If the user hasn't selected an OS preference, the theme defaults to light mode. The user can switch between light and dark mode using the toggle in the nav. Dark/light theme preferences are stored on the client, in <code>localStorage.theme</code></p>\n<p>Adding new dark classes is easy. Simply prefix the Tailwind CSS class name with <code>dark:</code> and it will be applied during dark mode only.</p>\n<h5>Google Analytics</h5>\n<p>Understanding traffic is a big part of blogging. Simply copy your Google ID, paste it into the <code>.env.local</code> file, and you're all set. The Google tag is set up to track a number of events, including form submissions.</p>\n<h5>A working contact form</h5>\n<p>The contact form uses <a href=\"https://sendgrid.com/\">SendGrid</a> to send emails from your contact form on your behalf. Their free plan is suitable. Just add your SendGrid API key, and your \"to\" and \"from\" email addresses to the <code>.env.local</code> file, and you're all set. Submissions to the contact form will be emailed directly to your inbox. And, as mentioned above, all submissions on the contact form are tracked as Google Analytics events.</p>\n<h5>Markdown</h5>\n<p>It's a blog. We're here for the content. You shouldn't be wrangling HTML or CSS. You should be writing. Creating. That's it. And that's exactly what markdown lets you do. But, unlike many blog starters touting markdown, <em>this</em> starter actually comes with built-in styling. Take a look at the <a href=\"/posts/markdown-guide\">Markdown Guide</a> for examples.</p>\n<h5>Syntax highlighting</h5>\n<p>Is your blog code heavy? Tutorials? All preformatted (<code>&#x3C;pre></code>) code blocks are highlighted with Prism. The theme can be adjusted in the Meta component.</p>\n<h5>Tailwind CSS</h5>\n<p>Tailwind keeps CSS manageable, in an understandable, and scalable way. If you're not already familiar with Tailwind, check it out <a href=\"https://tailwindcss.com\">here</a>, and never look back.</p>\n<h5>Storybook</h5>\n<p>Storybook helps you build more composable components, by crafting and testing them in isolation. Run <code>yarn storybook</code> from the root directory to start Storybook and view stories.</p>\n<h5>Statically generated</h5>\n<p>Need to show the same page to a bunch of users? Generate it at build. When your users ask for your content, deliver it lightning fast.</p>\n<h5>Image optimization</h5>\n<p>Defer images you don't need, and prioritize the ones you do. All the while, making sure you're serving up the smallest size possible without sacrificing quality. For example, the hero image on the homepage receives priority (since it's above the fold), while the images below the fold, in the \"More stories\" section, will be deferred. The same is true for the hero image on each blog post's page.</p>\n<h5>React</h5>\n<p>A fast, robust library. Popular enough that you'll always be able to find the tools and packages you need to build out custom features.</p>\n","ogImage":{"url":"/assets/blog/a-nextjs-blog-starter-you-actually-want-to-use/tree.jpg"},"coverImage":"/assets/blog/a-nextjs-blog-starter-you-actually-want-to-use/tree-minimal.jpg"}},"__N_SSG":true}}